  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nNot much change to the forecast with rain chances in the morning\nand then again with the afternoon seabreeze on Thursday. Rainfall\namounts so far with today's precip has generally remained under\n0.5 inch with 1-2 inch amounts with locations seeing multiple\nshowers/storms and any stronger ones. \n\nPREVIOUS DISCUSSION...\n\nA mid level inverted trough of low pressure is forecast to move over \nS TX today, which will provide mid/upper level support for \nconvective development, mainly along the daily sea breeze. With \nthe deeper moisture and the weakest capping inversion expected \nacross the Victoria Crossroads, probabilities will be highest up \nto 60% with lower probabilities south and west. The lowest chance \nwill be across the Rio Grande Plains. Rainfall totals Through \nthrough Friday are expected to be generally less than 0.5 inch, \nbut with PWATs of 2.0-2.3 inches, a few stronger storms may \nproduce 1-2 inches. Rain chances are progged to decrease through \nthe weekend as mid level ridging builds back across S TX. \n\nWith the ridge comes hotter temperatures and higher heat indices. An \nExtreme risk of heat related impacts are forecast across most of S \nTX by the weekend. Although, heat indices up to 113 may occur Thursday,\nit is expected to be isolated with short duration, thus Heat \nAdvisories are less likely to be needed. However, by Friday and \ninto the weekend the duration of heat indices of 110+ across a \nlarger area may warrant daily Heat Advisories, possibly into early\nnext week.",
